INT. HOSPITAL STAIRWELL - LANDING BETWEEN FLOORS - 3 A.M.

Concrete steps. A humming exit sign. A window onto a dark parking structure. A vending machine glows at the landing.

DR. ANSELMO KWAKU-REYNOLDS, 40s, scrubs, paper hat crumpled in his fist, sits on the steps with his head against the wall. A phone rests on his knee, speaker on.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(distant, tired)
Cardiology to Dr. Kwaku-Reynolds. Please call extension four-four-one-two.

ANSELMO
(to the phone)
I'm on a break.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
Doctor, they said it's urgent.

ANSELMO
It's always urgent.

He turns the phone face down on the step. The voice continues in muffled bursts. He lets it.

ANSELMO (CONT'D)
(to himself, steady)
Forty-one patients on the unit. Three discharges at eight. Two transfers at noon. One bed that nobody gets until I sign off on a chart I haven't finished. And I'm sitting on a stair.

He laughs, quietly. Wipes a hand over his face.

ANSELMO (CONT'D)
When I was a student, an old attending told me: "A doctor is a man who fixes what he can and carries what he can't." I thought it was poetry.

A pause. The vending machine hums.

ANSELMO (CONT'D)
It's a weight limit.

He stands, brushes off his scrubs, flattens the paper hat, puts it on. Picks up the phone.

ANSELMO (CONT'D)
(into the phone)
This is Kwaku-Reynolds. Coming up.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
Thank you, Doctor.

He pockets the phone and climbs.
